<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau, City of Refuge National Historical Park</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park, on the west coast of the island of Hawaiʻi, preserves a puʻuhonua, a place of refuge where until the early 19th century Hawaiians who had broken a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> could escape death, be absolved by a priest and go free; defeated warriors and non-combatants could also shelter there in war. Its Hale o Keawe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">heiau</a>, built about 1650 as the burial place of the ruler Keaweʻīkekahialiʻiokamoku, held 23 remains; the last bones were removed in 1829 and the temple destroyed, then reconstructed in the 1960s. Established in 1955 as City of Refuge National Historical Park, the 420-acre site took its Hawaiian spelling in 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pu-uhonua-o-honaunau-national-historical-park.jpg" alt="Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park" width="330" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carol M. Highsmith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puʻuhonua_o_Hōnaunau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
